1.49.s:
// nidāgha uvāca
bho vipra janasaṃmarddo mahāneṣa janeśvare /
pravivakṣau pure ramye tenātra sthīyate mayā //

1.50.m:
He should not force the speech, nor should he suppress his breath;
by suppression of the breath, discordance of voice is produced,
and the sweetness of vowels and consonants is destroyed—there is no doubt of this.

Thus has the treatise on phonetics been indicated to you by me in brief outline;
by knowing this foremost limb of the Veda, one is prepared for becoming Brahman.

Thus ends the fiftieth chapter in the second quarter of the Bṛhadupākhyāna in the Pūrvabhāga of the glorious Bṛhannāradīyapurāṇa.

1.51.m:
Afflicted by him, a prince does not gain kingship;
a maiden gains no husband, a woman obtains no child or pregnancy,
a Vedic scholar attains no preceptor status, and a pupil attains no study.

A merchant obtains no profit, and a farmer gains no crop.
A bathing rite should be performed for him on an auspicious day according to the rules;
with a paste of white mustard, auspicious words should be recited by brāhmaṇas.
